;; PROMPT CHAINS — which text a prompt layer resolves to, as data.
;;
;; Ported from llm-repl's preamble chain (roster/resolve-preamble), whose rule
;; is FIRST-PRESENT-WINS: a level REPLACES the text, never concatenates it. An
;; absent level inherits from the one below; a level that is present but blank
;; means "explicitly none" and stops the walk.
;;
;; That is the semantics samizdat was missing. A project could add to the
;; system prompt (a manifest's :prompt is appended, deliberately, so a
;; specialised workflow keeps the whole documented tool surface) but had no way
;; to REPLACE it or to suppress it — the shipped prompt was the floor whatever
;; the project was.
;;
;; Entry kinds, and what makes each "present":
;;
;;   {:project "path"}  a file relative to the working directory — the project
;;                      being worked on. Present when the file exists. Blank
;;                      content means explicitly none. Same override
;;                      convention as .samizdat/cells.
;;   {:file "name"}     resources/prompts/name.md, through io/resource, so it
;;                      works inside a built binary. Present when it resolves.
;;   {:text "..."}      a literal. Present unless blank.
;;
;; Editing this file changes which text the harness boots with, at runtime,
;; with no rebuild.
{;; The base system prompt. A project drops its own system.md in
 ;; .samizdat/prompts/ to replace the shipped one outright — for a codebase
 ;; whose conventions are nothing like the ones system.md teaches — or leaves
 ;; that file empty to boot with no base prompt at all and let a workflow's
 ;; own :prompt be the whole instruction set.
 :system [{:project ".samizdat/prompts/system.md"}
          {:file "system"}]}
